You scan the equation from left to right.

04:35.980 --> 04:41.100
You look for the innermost parentheses, which in this specific case is the five times four.

04:41.580 --> 04:47.020
You evaluate that tiny piece to 20, and then you replace that whole chunk of text with the new number.

04:47.020 --> 04:51.240
And then you step outward to the next set of parentheses, and you do it again.

04:51.240 --> 05:03.180
Right, but this text argues that this simple, mundane, everyday algorithm, locating a scope, evaluating the math inside it, and replacing it, is not just a trick for kids learning arithmetic.

05:03.480 --> 05:04.800
No, it's much bigger than that.

05:05.080 --> 05:10.060
Fuchsian claims this is the true, underlying, primitive operation of all computation.

05:10.460 --> 05:13.360
What's fascinating here is how we normally treat those parentheses.

05:13.360 --> 05:18.280
I mean, in traditional computer science, parentheses are viewed merely as temporary syntax.

05:18.480 --> 05:19.120
Like punctuation.

05:19.400 --> 05:19.840
Exactly.

05:20.040 --> 05:21.260
They are punctuation marks.

05:21.420 --> 05:25.740
They are just there to tell the compiler or the human reader what to do first.

05:26.220 --> 05:31.780
But once the math inside them is finished, the parentheses themselves simply disappear.

05:31.900 --> 05:32.520
They just vanish.

05:32.520 --> 05:35.660
The steps are completely forgotten, and the boundary vanishes.

05:36.440 --> 05:50.880
But the sphere pop calculus, which Flyxion abbreviates throughout the paper as SPC, by the way, it argues that real computation is not about manipulating these temporary textual symbols on a flat, two-dimensional page.

05:51.180 --> 05:51.960
So what is it about?

05:52.260 --> 05:56.000
It's about creating persistent geometric regions of history.

05:56.240 --> 05:57.520
Geometric regions of history.

05:57.520 --> 06:03.600
I remember reading that line in the abstract and thinking it sounded like a concept from, I don't know, a hard sci-fi novel.

06:03.720 --> 06:04.860
It does sound a bit sci-fi.

06:05.020 --> 06:09.060
But as you dig into the pages, it is incredibly tangibly practical.

06:09.420 --> 06:21.020
We're going to take a journey today, moving step-by-step from that simple grade school math problem into a radical new geometry of computing made up of what the text calls spheres and pops.

06:21.240 --> 06:22.080
Spheres and pops, yep.

06:22.080 --> 06:37.460
We're going to explore how this new geometry handles things that would normally crash our computers, how it unleashes true concurrency without the multi-threading nightmares software developers usually face, and, you know, how it deals with probability.

06:37.640 --> 06:38.780
Which is a huge deal.

06:38.780 --> 06:52.960
And finally, we will uncover the theoretical universal operator machine, which is an engine that could completely redefine how future compilers and artificial intelligence runtimes actually operate under the hood.

06:53.300 --> 06:54.900
It is a complete paradigm shift.

06:55.440 --> 07:00.320
To really grasp it, we have to willingly suspend our current understanding of programming.

07:00.460 --> 07:01.900
We have to kind of unlearn everything.

