07:30.080 --> 07:36.260
Take the expression 2 plus 3 times 4 and compare it to, in parentheses, 2 plus 3 times 4.

07:36.560 --> 07:38.680
They contain the exact same mathematical symbols.

07:38.820 --> 07:39.960
The numbers are identical.

07:40.380 --> 07:43.280
The plus sign and the multiplication sign are identical.

07:43.460 --> 07:44.880
Yep, the raw materials are the same.

07:45.000 --> 07:51.480
But adding those two curved lines, the parentheses, fundamentally changes the reality and the outcome of the computation.

07:52.200 --> 07:55.640
And yet, the parentheses themselves don't actually do any math.

07:55.720 --> 07:57.360
Right, a parenthesis doesn't add.

07:57.420 --> 07:59.400
No, it doesn't multiply, it doesn't divide.

07:59.400 --> 08:01.440
What it does is pure organization.

08:01.940 --> 08:02.420
Precisely.

08:02.680 --> 08:05.880
They establish a temporary computational environment.

08:06.140 --> 08:13.540
If you think about traditional computing paradigms, whether you are looking at something as old and foundational as the lambda calculus,

08:13.820 --> 08:19.520
or a modern programming language like Python, Java, or C++ Suite.

08:19.620 --> 08:21.180
We use those brackets all the time.

08:21.180 --> 08:24.620
Yeah, we use brackets, braces, and parentheses to create these boundaries.

08:24.620 --> 08:33.740
For the listener who might not be a computer science major, the lambda calculus is basically a mathematical framework developed in the 1930s by Alonzo Church.

08:33.840 --> 08:34.720
So, one of the pioneers.

08:35.200 --> 08:35.800
Exactly.

08:36.220 --> 08:42.960
It treats all computation as just plugging variables into functions over and over, replacing terms with new terms.

08:42.960 --> 08:53.100
And in all these systems from the 30s to today, we use these textual markers to define a boundary where certain rules or operations take priority.

08:53.100 --> 08:59.540
But the illusion, Flyxion points out, is that we have tricked ourselves into thinking that computation is entirely about the symbols.

08:59.720 --> 08:59.900
Right.

09:00.000 --> 09:00.080
Yes.

09:00.560 --> 09:05.380
The numbers and the operators, rather than the spaces those symbols inhabit.

09:05.620 --> 09:05.820
Right.

09:05.820 --> 09:15.060
If I go back to that student at the whiteboard, you write 2 plus 3, you calculate 5, and you literally take an eraser, wipe away the 2 plus 3, and write 5 in its place.

09:15.380 --> 09:17.460
The history of that addition is gone.

09:17.700 --> 09:18.660
Completely erased.

09:18.880 --> 09:27.480
The computational boundary that held the 2 plus 3 together existed for a brief, fleeting second to serve a purpose, and then it was utterly destroyed to make room for the present.

09:27.480 --> 09:33.740
And, you know, to be fair to 70 years of computer science, that amnesiac approach works perfectly fine for simple arithmetic.

09:34.140 --> 09:35.240
It saves space.

09:35.340 --> 09:35.540
Yeah.

09:35.680 --> 09:39.420
But the sphere pop calculus asks a critical what-if question.

09:40.500 --> 09:45.960
What if we stop interpreting these parentheses as textual delimiters on a flat page?

09:46.320 --> 09:52.380
What if we stop seeing them as ink on paper, and instead view them as explicit geometric objects?

09:52.560 --> 09:53.980
And this is where it gets visual.

09:54.260 --> 09:54.540
Right.

09:54.540 --> 09:58.640
Flyxion introduces a term for these objects, spheres.

09:59.500 --> 10:04.460
In STC, a sphere creates a literal, mathematically bounded region of computation.

10:05.140 --> 10:08.660
It is not just punctuation, it is a topological space.

10:08.820 --> 10:10.000
A topological space.

10:10.020 --> 10:14.420
Yeah, it has a definite impermeable inside and a definite separate outside.

10:14.780 --> 10:24.360
Wait, so instead of just reading a line of text left to right, I need to visualize bubbles, like physical bubbles floating in space and sometimes nested inside of each other.

10:24.360 --> 10:26.480
That is exactly the right visualization.

10:26.800 --> 10:32.720
When you open a parenthesis in this new calculus, you aren't just writing a curved line on a screen to tell the computer to wait.

10:32.920 --> 10:33.680
What are you doing then?

10:33.840 --> 10:37.120
You are geometrically inflating a new computational workspace.

10:37.400 --> 10:37.920
A sphere.

10:38.520 --> 10:46.900
Inside this boundary, local variables can be born, intermediate mathematical values can be created, and dependencies can interact with one another.

10:46.900 --> 10:47.580
Okay, I see.

10:47.900 --> 10:59.760
But crucially, everything happening inside that sphere is completely, perfectly isolated from the outside computational world until the work inside is completely finished.

11:00.440 --> 11:01.780
I'm trying to picture this.

11:02.280 --> 11:06.420
If I have a sphere for five times four, all the multiplying happens in that bubble.

11:06.420 --> 11:11.740
The rest of the equation, the three times on the outside, has absolutely no idea what is going on inside the bubble.

11:11.860 --> 11:12.380
None at all.

11:12.460 --> 11:14.560
It just sees the opaque surface of the bubble.

11:14.800 --> 11:23.140
And when the math inside is finally finished, that brings us to the core mechanism, and frankly, my favorite term in this whole dense paper, the pop.

11:23.540 --> 11:24.420
It's a great term.

11:24.420 --> 11:34.220
When a sphere's internal dependencies are totally resolved, when it's done all the logic it was asked to do and holds the final value, it reaches a state the text calls ready.

11:34.940 --> 11:36.800
And when it is ready, it pops.

11:37.020 --> 11:43.120
The underlying mechanics of that pop are absolutely vital to understand if we want to see why this is different from traditional cod.

11:43.360 --> 11:44.260
So how is it different?

11:44.580 --> 11:48.340
Well, in traditional programming, evaluation is essentially a string rewrite rule.

11:48.460 --> 11:53.280
You find a string of text, like five times four, and you substitute it with a new string of text, 20.

11:53.280 --> 11:55.300
It is a textual replacement.

11:55.740 --> 11:58.120
Right, like crossing out a word and writing a new one above it.

11:58.180 --> 11:58.660
Exactly.

11:59.480 --> 12:03.340
But a pop in the sphere pop calculus is a geometric event.

12:03.920 --> 12:07.480
It is the literal mathematical dissolution of a boundary.

12:08.200 --> 12:19.720
The sphere has fulfilled its purpose, so the membrane separating the inside from the outside vanishes, exposing the interior result to the surrounding computational landscape.

12:20.080 --> 12:21.360
Like a biological cell.

12:21.460 --> 12:22.780
Oh, that's a good comparison.

12:22.780 --> 12:26.580
Yeah, think of a cell membrane dissolving to release a protein it just manufactured.

12:27.000 --> 12:39.420
The outside environment couldn't touch or interfere with what was happening inside the cell while it was working, but the exact moment it pops, the finished protein is suddenly available for the next larger biological process to use.

12:39.420 --> 12:48.380
If we connect this to the bigger picture, this exact moment, the pop, is where the narrative of traditional computation and the sphere pop calculus diverge completely.

12:48.640 --> 12:48.980
How so?

12:49.200 --> 12:55.940
In traditional computing, when that expression evaluates and the boundary vanishes, the memory of the boundary vanishes right along with it.

12:55.940 --> 12:59.400
The computer sweeps the discarded scope into the trash.

12:59.740 --> 13:01.260
It reclaims the memory.

13:01.460 --> 13:02.460
Right, to free up RAM.

13:02.840 --> 13:10.620
As we noted, it is an incredibly efficient way to save RAM, but Flyxion argues it comes at a massive, often catastrophic semantic cost.

13:10.620 --> 13:14.420
Which is the perfect bridge to our next major realization.

13:14.420 --> 13:21.440
Because if computation is just a series of geometric spheres popping, what actually happens to the pop sphere?

13:21.760 --> 13:23.240
That is the million dollar question.

13:23.240 --> 13:31.660
Because SBC's absolute, stubborn refusal to forget the past fundamentally changes the nature of what data even is.

13:31.800 --> 13:37.300
This brings us to what the monograph calls the missing object of traditional computer science.

13:37.380 --> 13:38.180
The missing object.

13:38.380 --> 13:38.600
Right.

13:39.140 --> 13:46.820
In historical systems, again, going back to Turing machines or the lambda calculus, the current state of the machine is the only thing that matters.

13:46.820 --> 13:54.560
A Turing machine, conceptualized by Alan Turing, is basically a theoretical machine that reads a long tape of ones and zeros.

13:54.660 --> 13:55.680
Right, moving back and forth.

13:55.780 --> 13:56.140
Exactly.

13:56.560 --> 14:00.300
It moves left or right, it reads a symbol, it overwrites it with a new symbol, and moves on.

14:00.780 --> 14:04.520
The machine lives entirely exclusively in the present tense.

14:04.680 --> 14:06.660
It only knows what is on the tape right now.

14:06.960 --> 14:09.080
The text calls this out so aggressively.

14:09.660 --> 14:12.760
It says traditional languages preserve only the current state.

14:12.760 --> 14:17.860
Once 2 plus 3 becomes 5, the fact that it used to be 2 plus 3 is gone.

14:18.120 --> 14:18.600
Vanished.

14:18.820 --> 14:23.800
But SBC argues that the current state is not the most fundamental object of computing.

14:24.400 --> 14:31.860
The fundamental primitive, the basic building block of all logic, is the irreversible history of how that state came to be.

14:32.080 --> 14:34.480
Think about the magnitude of that shift for a moment.

14:35.040 --> 14:40.060
The program is no longer a sequence of isolated transient states replacing each other.

14:40.060 --> 14:45.040
It is the construction of a permanent, ever-expanding historical record.

14:45.200 --> 14:46.160
A permanent record.

14:46.280 --> 14:46.600
Yes.

14:47.100 --> 14:54.060
Every single sphere that inflates, every dependency that resolves inside it, every pop that dissolves a boundary, all of it is recorded.

14:54.540 --> 14:56.080
The computation only grows.

14:56.320 --> 14:58.180
It never, ever overwrites.

14:58.240 --> 14:59.600
Okay, wait, I have to push back here.

14:59.700 --> 14:59.860
Sure.

14:59.860 --> 15:08.620
I am putting myself in the shoes of a software engineer listening to this, or really anyone who has ever had their web browser crash because it used up too much memory.

